module Darklang.Cli.Docs.HttpClient

let content(): String =
  """# HTTP Client

## Requests

  Stdlib.HttpClient.get(url, [ ("Accept", "application/json") ])
  Stdlib.HttpClient.post(url, [ Stdlib.HttpClient.ContentType.json ], body)
  Stdlib.HttpClient.request("PATCH", url, headers, body)   # any method; body is Bytes
  Stdlib.HttpClient.stream("GET", url, headers, Stdlib.Bytes.empty)

Each answers `Result<Response<'body>, RequestError>`:

  type Stdlib.HttpClient.Response<'body> =
    { statusCode: Stdlib.Http.Status, headers: List<String * String>, body: 'body }

`body` is Bytes from `request`, `get` and the rest, and a lazy `Stream<UInt8>`
from `stream`. Headers come back as the server sent them, names and values,
and a header sent twice (`Set-Cookie`) is two pairs. Look one up whatever its
case:

  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.header(r, "ETag")       # Some("\"AbC\"")
  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.headers(r, "Set-Cookie") # every value
  Stdlib.HttpClient.Response.text(r)                  # body as UTF-8, or Decode

A GET or HEAD with no body is a read: it runs while your program goes on, and
waits only where you look at the response, so `List.map(urls, get)` has them
all in flight at once, up to 50 connections to any one host; the rest wait
their turn. A stream holds its connection until it is drained or closed, and
counts against the 50 until then. Every other request runs in program order.

Redirects are followed, up to 10, as curl -L and fetch do: a POST after a 301
or 302, and anything but a GET or HEAD after a 303, becomes a GET; otherwise
the method and body are kept. `Authorization`, `Cookie` and
`Proxy-Authorization` stay behind when one leaves the origin. One more is
`TooManyRedirects`.

## Middleware

A request is a record, and sending is a function:

  type Request = { method, url, headers, body,
                   timeoutMs: Option<Int64>, redirects: Option<Int64>,
                   retry: Option<RetryPolicy>, originHeaders }
  type Send<'body> = Request -> Result<Response<'body>, RequestError>
  type Middleware<'body> = Send<'body> -> Send<'body>

`get` and friends send through `Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.defaults()`: trim
header names, refuse an empty one, and follow up to 10 redirects. Build your
own client from a list, first listed outermost. For `timeout`,
`followRedirects` and `retry` the one listed last wins, so
`List.append(Middleware.defaults(), Middleware.followRedirects(0))` follows
none; a value you set on the request yourself wins over them all:

  let send =
    Stdlib.HttpClient.client(
      Stdlib.List.flatten(
        [ Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.defaults()
          [ Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.baseUrl("https://api.example.com/v1/")
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.bearer("https://api.example.com", token)
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.timeout(5000)
            Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware.retry(3, 200) ] ]))

  send(Stdlib.HttpClient.Request.make("GET", "users", [], Stdlib.Bytes.empty))

`client([])` is the raw builtin, `Stdlib.HttpClient.sendRaw`, which reshapes
nothing and follows no redirect; `streamClient` is the same for streams.

  Middleware.onRequest(f)        rewrite every request
  Middleware.onResponse(f)       rewrite every response, without waiting; f only computes
  Middleware.header(n, v)        add a header unless the request has one
  Middleware.timeout(ms)         the exchange in ms, or `Timeout`; 0 is none
  Middleware.baseUrl(base)       resolve relative urls against base
  Middleware.bearer(origin, t)   Authorization: Bearer, to that origin only,
                                 judged by the url it is finally sent to
  Middleware.followRedirects(n)  up to n redirects, then `TooManyRedirects`
  Middleware.retry(n, ms)        n attempts in all, idempotent methods only
  Middleware.lowercaseNames()    response header names in lowercase

`retry` tries again on a failure to connect and on 429, 502, 503 or 504. It
waits what `Retry-After` asks, or else `ms` doubled each time with some
jitter, never more than a minute, and gives up when the wait would pass the
timeout. The timeout covers every attempt and every redirect: all of a
buffered response, and up to the last response's headers for a stream. The
host follows the redirects and makes the retries, so a GET through them is
still in flight.

A middleware is any function from a `Send` to a `Send`, so your own is a few
lines, used as `logged()` in the list:

  let logged(): Stdlib.HttpClient.Middleware<Bytes> =
    fun next ->
      fun req ->
        let r = next(req)
        let status =
          match r with
          | Ok(response) -> Stdlib.display(response.statusCode)
          | Error(e) -> Stdlib.HttpClient.toString(e)
        Stdlib.printLine(req.method + " " + req.url + " " + status)
        r

`logged` looks at the response, so it waits for it where it is sent: a GET
through it is no longer in flight while your program goes on. `onResponse`
does not wait: its function runs when the response arrives, apart from your
program, so it may only compute, for a POST as for a GET. A print there fails
the request; log where you use the response, or in your own middleware like
`logged`.

## What middleware cannot do

Your grants, the network guard, and the refusal of CR, LF or NUL in a header
are the host's, below every middleware. Each redirect and retry is a fresh
send, checked again for wherever it goes.

## Errors

  BadUrl(...)  Timeout  BadHeader(...)  NetworkError  TlsError(msg)  BadMethod
  TooManyRedirects  Decode(msg)

`Stdlib.HttpClient.toString(e)` says one in words.
"""
